    date: "July 28, 2026",
    excerpt: "Department of Supply Chain and Automation Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, participates in the Regional Consultation on Biochar and Alternate Wetting and Drying Irrigation for Climate-Smart Rice Production in ASEAN 📍 Asian Institute of Technology (AIT), Thailand Ms. Sreyneath CHHUN, Lecturer of the Department of Supply Chain and Automation Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Royal University of Phnom Penh, participated as a Supply Chain Researcher in the Regional Consultation on Biochar and Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) Irrigation for Climate-Smart Rice Production in ASEAN, held on November 07th, 2025, at Asian Institute of Technology (AIT). This regional consultation aims to engage more than 150 participants from across the ASEAN region — including Cambodia, Lao PDR, and Thailand — comprising farmers and key stakeholders involved in rice production and the value chain.",
    badge: "RUPP",
    author: "Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering",
    readTime: "4 min read",
    tags: ["NEWS","News","Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering"],
    body: [
      { heading: "Overview", paragraphs: ["Ruppers Abroad · Regional Consultation At AIT: our lecturer joins the ASEAN consultation on climate-smart rice production Ms. Sreyneath Chhun, Lecturer of the Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Royal University of Phnom Penh, took part as a Supply Chain Researcher in the Regional Consultation on Biochar and Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) Irrigation for Climate-Smart Rice Production in ASEAN, held on 07 November 2025 at the Asian Institute of Technology (AIT), Thailand. 07 November 2025 Regional Consultation Asian Institute of Technology · Thailand Participation A supply chain voice in an ASEAN conversation about rice The Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Royal University of Phnom Penh, was represented at the Regional Consultation on Biochar and Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) Irrigation for Climate-Smart Rice Production in ASEAN, hosted by the Department of Food, Agriculture and Natural Resources of the Asian Institute of Technology. Ms. Sreyneath Chhun, Lecturer of the department, joined the consultation as a Supply Chain Researcher — bringing the logistics and value chain perspective to a discussion otherwise centred on agronomy, soil science and irrigation practice. The consultation set out to engage more than 150 participants from across the ASEAN region — including Cambodia, Lao PDR and Thailand — comprising farmers and key stakeholders involved in rice production and the rice value chain. The Consultation Regional Consultation on Biochar and Alternate Wetting and Drying Irrigation for Climate-Smart Rice Production in ASEAN Department of Food, Agriculture and Natural Resources, AIT · 07 November 2025 Robert B Banks Auditorium, AIT Conference Center (AITCC), Asian Institute of Technology, Thailand. Ms. Sreyneath Chhun at the Regional Consultation, 07 November 2025, Asian Institute of Technology, Thailand. The Robert B Banks Auditorium at the AIT Conference Center during the day’s working sessions. About the Consultation Two field practices, one regional supply chain Rice is the staple of the ASEAN region and one of its largest sources of agricultural greenhouse gas emissions. The consultation brought farmers, researchers and value chain stakeholders together around two practices that can lower those emissions without lowering yields — and around the question of what it takes to move them from trial plots into everyday practice. 01 Biochar A charcoal-like material made from rice husk and other crop residues. Worked into the soil, it improves soil quality and holds carbon in the ground instead of releasing it. 02 Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) An irrigation method that lets a paddy field dry out between floodings rather than keeping it permanently submerged. It cuts water use and the methane a flooded field gives off. 03 The rice value chain Whether either practice spreads depends on the chain around the field — residue collection, input supply, milling, storage and the route to market. This is where the department’s supply chain expertise contributes. Key takeaway Climate-smart rice is not only an agronomy problem. Getting biochar and AWD into ordinary practice across Cambodia, Lao PDR and Thailand is equally a supply chain problem — and that is the perspective the Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering brought to the table. At the Event Participants and delegates at AIT Delegates receive their certificates of participation on the auditorium stage. Ms. Sreyneath Chhun (far left) with fellow participants at the certificate presentation. Participants of the Regional Consultation on Biochar and AWD Irrigation for Climate-Smart Rice Production in ASEAN. Representing the Department Our delegate Ms. Sreyneath Chhun Lecturer · Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Royal University of Phnom Penh Supply Chain Researcher At a Glance Key details Item Detail Event Regional Consultation on Biochar and Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) Irrigation for Climate-Smart Rice Production in ASEAN Date 07 November 2025 Venue Robert B Banks Auditorium, AIT Conference Center (AITCC), Asian Institute of Technology (AIT), Thailand Organiser Department of Food, Agriculture and Natural Resources, Asian Institute of Technology RUPP representative Ms. Sreyneath Chhun, Lecturer, Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering Role Supply Chain Researcher Participants More than 150 from across the ASEAN region Countries represented Cambodia, Lao PDR and Thailand Stakeholders Farmers and key stakeholders involved in rice production and the rice value chain Focus Biochar and Alternate Wetting and Drying irrigation for climate-smart rice production Acknowledgement “Regional platforms like this one are where our teaching meets the problems the region is actually solving — and where our staff bring a supply chain perspective back into the classroom.” Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering Faculty of Engineering · Royal University of Phnom Penh"] },
    ]
