    title: "Two teams in Ho Chi Minh City, one prize home: SCA at the Humanitarian Logistics Hackathon",
    date: "July 28, 2026",
    excerpt: "We are proud to share that on 28–29 March, students from the Department of Supply Chain and Automation Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Royal University of Phnom Penh, join the Humanitarian Logistics Hackathon in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam. This event is organized by the Kühne Logistics University with participants from Cambodia, Germany, and Vietnam. There are 10 students, sent from the RUPP and divided into two teams, including (1) Chain Master and (2) Link 4 Life. Congratulations to both teams on this wonderful achievement. Thank you for representing our department with passion, teamwork, and commitment to bring the prize back to RUPP. You have truly made us proud on the international stage.",
    badge: "RUPP",
    author: "Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering",
    readTime: "5 min read",
    tags: ["NEWS","Activity","Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering"],
    body: [
      { heading: "Overview", paragraphs: ["Ruppers Abroad · International Competition Two teams in Ho Chi Minh City, one prize home: SCA at the Humanitarian Logistics Hackathon We are proud to share that on 28–29 March 2026, ten students from the Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Royal University of Phnom Penh, joined the Humanitarian Logistics Hackathon in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam — an event organised by Kühne Logistics University with participants from Cambodia, Germany and Vietnam. 28–29 March 2026 International Hackathon Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam Congratulations Ten students, two teams, and a prize brought back to RUPP The Royal University of Phnom Penh sent ten students from the Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering (ASCSE), Faculty of Engineering, to the Humanitarian Logistics Hackathon in Ho Chi Minh City. They competed as two teams — Link 4 Life and Chain Master — over two days of problem-framing, prototyping and pitching. The hackathon was organised by Kühne Logistics University (Hamburg, Germany) together with the Business Startup Support Centre (BSSC) and innoex, with HELP Logistics as strategic partner, and drew student teams from Cambodia, Germany and Vietnam. The brief asked teams to design practical answers to real humanitarian logistics problems — getting relief goods, volunteers and information to the people who need them, on time and under pressure. Congratulations to both teams on this wonderful achievement. Thank you for representing our department with passion, teamwork and commitment, and for bringing the prize back to RUPP. You have truly made us proud on the international stage. Result Consolation Prize, University Track — Humanitarian Logistics Hackathon 2026 Team Link 4 Life · Award Ceremony, Ho Chi Minh City · 29 March 2026 5,000,000 VND Total prize value, as shown on the award cheque. The full RUPP delegation — both teams together at the hackathon venue, Ho Chi Minh City, 28–29 March 2026. Link 4 Life and Chain Master between sessions during the two-day hackathon. The Delegation Link 4 Life and Chain Master 01 Link 4 Life Consolation Prize · University Track Link 4 Life pitched a coordination dashboard for an NGO workspace — tracking action requests, completed deliveries and engaged volunteers alongside a live view of goods in stock and supply against demand. The team took the Consolation Prize in the University Track, worth 5,000,000 VND. Link 4 Life presenting their NGO workspace dashboard to the judges. With the prize cheque, the department banner and the Cambodian flag after the award ceremony. Working through the brief on the hackathon floor. 02 Chain Master University Track Chain Master carried the department's second entry through the same two days of mentoring, prototyping and pitching, and stood on the award-ceremony stage alongside the teams from Cambodia, Germany and Vietnam. Their name was read out from the stage as one of the two Royal University of Phnom Penh teams in the competition. Chain Master at work with a mentor during the hackathon. Receiving their participation gifts on stage at the award ceremony. About the Hackathon Logistics, but for people in crisis Humanitarian logistics is the work of moving relief goods, volunteers and information to people affected by disaster or hardship — the same planning, routing and inventory problems taught in a supply chain classroom, but with lives rather than margins at stake. Over two days in Ho Chi Minh City, mixed university teams were given a humanitarian brief, mentored by academics and industry practitioners, and asked to pitch a working answer to a judging panel. 01 Two days, one brief Teams framed the problem, built a prototype and rehearsed a pitch inside a 28–29 March window — the compressed timeline is the point of a hackathon. 02 Three countries at one table Participants came from Cambodia, Germany and Vietnam, so every team worked and presented in English and Vietnam across three academic cultures. 03 Judged on stage Each team pitched live before a panel, with results announced at an award ceremony attended by the organisers, partners and all competing universities. Key takeaway Sending two full teams — not one — put ten SCA students in front of an international panel, and one of them came home with a prize. That is the department's supply chain curriculum tested against a real humanitarian brief, in English, against universities from three countries. Award Ceremony On stage with Cambodia, Germany and Vietnam The closing award ceremony. Both RUPP teams were welcomed to the stage alongside Kühne Logistics University and the other competing universities; gifts were presented to the international contestants by Ms. Nguyễn Thị Diệu Hằng, Director of the Business Startup Support Centre (BSSC) and Vice President of the Ho Chi Minh City Young Business Association (YBA), with Prof. Dr. Andreas Kaplan, President of Kühne Logistics University, on stage. At a Glance Key details Item Detail Event Humanitarian Logistics Hackathon Dates 28–29 March 2026 Venue Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam Organiser Kühne Logistics University (KLU), Hamburg, Germany Organising committee Business Startup Support Centre (BSSC) · Kühne Logistics University · innoex Strategic partner HELP Logistics Participating countries Cambodia, Germany, Vietnam RUPP delegation 10 students, Department of Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering Teams (1) Link 4 Life · (2) Chain Master Result Link 4 Life — Consolation Prize, University Track (5,000,000 VND) Award ceremony Prof. Dr. Andreas Kaplan, President of Kühne Logistics University · Ms. Nguyễn Thị Diệu Hằng, Director of BSSC and Vice President of the Ho Chi Minh City Young Business Association (YBA) Acknowledgement “Thank you for representing our department with passion, teamwork and commitment, and for bringing the prize back to RUPP. You have truly made us proud on the international stage.” Automation & Supply Chain Systems Engineering Faculty of Engineering · Royal University of Phnom Penh"] },
    ]
